{"role":"assistant","content":"Fixed. The problem and the change:\n\n**Root cause** — `build_system()` in bot.py:130 sent the system prompt as one text block with a single `cache_control` marker. Since the cache is a prefix hash, every `save_fact` appended a fact to the tail, invalidated the whole block, and forced the next call to re-create the full ~1000-token prompt — exactly the `cache_read: 0` / `cache_creation` ~1000 pattern in `logs/usage.jsonl`.\n\n**The fix** — two blocks, each with its own `cache_control: {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}` marker:\n- Block 1: `INSTRUCTIONS + \"\\n\\n\"` — a module constant, byte-stable across users, days, and memory writes, so it stays cached.\n- Block 2: the volatile `athlete_context` tail (date, profile, zones, goal, facts, language).\n\nA memory write now re-caches only the ~100–250-token tail instead of the entire prompt. The rendered text is byte-for-byte what the old code sent, so model behavior is unchanged. `README.md`'s caching paragraph was updated to match, and the stale comment in `build_system` replaced.\n\n**Verified offline** (no external calls): two ephemeral-marked blocks; block 1 identical across athletes and dates; a simulated `save_fact` changes only block 2; rendered text matches the old assembly; end-to-end `reply()` with a stub LLM confirms the round after a write sends the identical static block. After deploy, the note's usage-log check applies: rounds after a `save_fact` should show `cache_read` covering the instructions and `cache_creation` at roughly tail size.\n\n**Note applied/ignored** — full detail is in `ANSWER.md`. The two-segment fix, byte-stability requirement, and `usage`-based verification applied directly; the \"leave keyword tool filtering alone, it's liveable\" judgment matched `NOTES.md`. Did not apply: the Telegram/MCP details, building tool filtering (already present), reordering the tail (cosmetic per the note itself), and live API verification (no key here, and external calls were out of bounds)."}
